  "image_name": "5d4f77c85953a95d04c3be4e14027c20.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts a present-day water cycle in a landscape with a projected future version under stronger atmospheric demand and human use. It shows rainfall feeding soil moisture, infiltration, runoff to a river, groundwater, soil evaporation, and plant transpiration. At leaf scale, the key comparison is between saturated and actual water vapour: the gap between them, labeled VPD, appears larger in the future panel. This larger vapor pressure deficit is linked to stomata exchanging less water vapor and more limited water status in plants.\n\nAcross the whole landscape, the future scene indicates several shifts with plus or minus symbols: higher precipitation, higher transpiration, higher soil evaporation, higher runoff, and greater human water demand, but lower soil moisture and reduced infiltration. Groundwater remains shown beneath the system, while rivers and runoff continue to connect hillslopes to downstream users. The industrial area and livestock emphasize added water withdrawals by people.\n\nOverall, the figure communicates that a warmer atmosphere increases evaporative demand, altering plant water loss and redistributing water through the land system, even where some inputs such as precipitation may also increase.",
